  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The reaction between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math>&nbsp;will takes place as:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>KI</mi><mo>&#10230;</mo><munder><mrow><msub><mi>CuI</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8595;</mo></mrow><mi>Unstable</mi></munder><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;a strong reducing agent, it reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;and a white precipitate of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cu</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is formed.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>CuI</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#10230;</mo><munder><mrow><msub><mi>Cu</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8595;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>White</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>Sodium thiosulphate is used as reducing agent to titrate iodine, with iodide being the product of the reaction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>NaI</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><munder><mrow><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></munder></math></p><p>So, the compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math>&nbsp;are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cu</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively.</p>"
